  "question": {
    "text": "Substance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> has atomic mass number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> and half life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> day. Another substance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> has atomic mass number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>32</mn></math> and half life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> day. If both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> simultaneously start undergo radio activity at the same time with initial mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>320</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> each, how many total atoms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> combined would be left after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> days"
  },
